    "nodes": [
      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "0512e62f-034e-5b6f-9c73-66075ccbc83b",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Borodin\n- Works: Stranger in Paradise, Polovtsian Dances, Prince Igor, Gliding Dance of the Maidens\n- Concepts: Polovtsy, Kipchaks, Cumans\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Borodin composed the opera Prince Igor, and the Polovtsian Dances form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of that opera.\n- Prince Igor is dated 1890 in this chunk.\n- The melody of the song Stranger in Paradise was taken from music by Alexander Borodin.\n- In Stranger in Paradise, the borrowed melody specifically came from Gliding Dance of the Maidens from the Polovtsian Dances in Prince Igor.\n- Polovtsian Dances is also spelled Polovetsian Dances.\n- The Russian title of Polovtsian Dances is Половецкие пляски, transliterated as Polovetskie plyaski.\n- The term Polovtsy was the name used by the Rus' people for the Kipchaks and Cumans.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "4f440921-78fc-554e-9ede-3d09eca03439",
        "text": "Page: Stranger in Paradise (song). Sentence: Like almost all the music in that show, the melody was taken from music composed by Alexander Borodin (1833–1887), in this case, the \"Gliding Dance of the Maidens\", from the \"Polovtsian Dances\" in the opera \"Prince Igor\" (1890).\nPage: Polovtsian Dances. Sentence: The Polovtsian Dances, or Polovetsian Dances (Russian: Половецкие пляски, \"Polovetskie plyaski\" from the Russian \"Polovtsy\"—the name given to the Kipchaks and Cumans by the Rus' people) form an exotic scene at the end of Act II of Alexander Borodin's opera \"Prince Igor\".",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
